{
  "id": "move-zeroes",
  "title": "Move Zeroes",
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "categories": ["two-pointers", "array"],
  "descriptionMd": "Rearrange the integer array `nums` **in place** so that every `0` ends up at the end, while the non-zero values keep the same relative order they had originally. Do not create a copy of the array; your function returns nothing.\n\n**Example 1**\n\n```\nnums = [0, 3, 0, 5, 8]\n→ nums becomes [3, 5, 8, 0, 0]\n```\n\n**Example 2**\n\n```\nnums = [4, 0, -2]\n→ nums becomes [4, -2, 0]\n```\n\n**Follow-up:** can you keep the number of writes to the array small?",
  "constraints": ["1 ≤ nums.length ≤ 10⁴", "-2³¹ ≤ nums[i] ≤ 2³¹ - 1"],
  "hints": ["Think of the non-zero values as being \"compacted\" toward the front. Where should the next non-zero value go?", "A second index that marks the next free slot for a non-zero value lets you do this in one pass."],
  "signature": {
    "functionName": "moveZeroes",
    "params": [{ "name": "nums", "type": "int[]" }],
    "returns": "void"
  },
  "comparator": "exact",
  "inPlaceArg": 0,
